# Secrets for the local Worker (`./cf npx wrangler dev`).
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# .dev.vars is git-ignored. Everything here is a SECRET — non-secret
# configuration belongs in the `vars` block of wrangler.jsonc, which is
# committed. In production these are set with:
#
#   ./cf npx wrangler secret put PAYLOAD_SECRET
#
# so the value reaches Cloudflare without passing through the
# repository or a deploy log.

# Signs sessions. There is deliberately no default: a value that changes
# on restart silently logs everyone out, and a shared value committed
# anywhere would let someone forge a session. Generate your own:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
PAYLOAD_SECRET=

# Send-to-Kindle. Optional: with this unset the feature is switched off
# cleanly — the account page says delivery is unavailable rather than
# offering a button that fails.
#
# Workers cannot speak SMTP, so delivery goes over Resend's HTTP API.
# The sending domain must be verified there first, and its DNS records
# added via `email_dns_records` in infra/terraform.tfvars — Amazon drops
# a personal document whose sender fails SPF or DKIM, silently.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Adobe PDF Services. Optional: with these unset, phase 1's export
# stages do not run and a queued scan simply waits — the same fail-closed
# shape the converter handoff uses, so the Worker can be deployed before
# the credentials exist.
#
# Both come from a project in the Adobe Developer Console
# (developer.adobe.com), under "PDF Services API". These are NOT the same
# thing as an Acrobat Pro subscription, which grants the desktop and web
# applications and no API credentials at all.
#
# Billing is per document transaction — one per 50 pages of a PDF → DOCX
# export, so a 400-page book costs 8. The free tier is 500 a month.
# ADOBE_API_KEY is accepted as an alias for ADOBE_CLIENT_ID, because that
# is what the Developer Console calls the value. The secret has no alias
# and is not optional: a token is issued from the pair, so the key on its
# own authenticates nothing.
ADOBE_CLIENT_ID=
ADOBE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The GraphQL playground at /api/graphql-playground. Local only.
#
# Not a secret — the one thing here that is not — but it belongs in this
# file rather than the committed `vars` block precisely because this file
# never reaches a deploy. Setting it turns on Payload's playground *and*
# schema introspection, and introspection is an ordinary query to
# /api/graphql that no role check can gate. The route itself is
# administrators-only either way.
#
# Unset it and the playground answers 404, which is the right state for
# anything deployed. The Swagger UI at /api/docs needs none of this.
PAYLOAD_GRAPHQL_PLAYGROUND=1
